<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeroboam II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 786–746 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeroboam II was king of Israel in the first half of the eighth century BCE, reigning about forty years according to the Books of Kings. Taking advantage of the weakness of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> after Assyrian attacks, he restored Israel's borders and presided over its greatest prosperity, probably reflected in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> ostraca. The prophets Amos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosea">Hosea</a> criticised the social injustice of his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeroboam-ii.jpg" alt="Jeroboam II" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume Rouille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeroboam_II.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Jeroboam II, also called Jeroboam son of Jehoash, succeeded Jehoash as the thirteenth king of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> and was the fourth king of the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>. He ruled for about four decades from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> and was the longest-reigning king of the northern kingdom; scholars date his reign differently, William F. Albright giving 786 to 746 BCE. His reign was contemporary with those of Amaziah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzziah">Uzziah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. He is described as a military commander who fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and he extended Israel to its former limits, from the entering of Hamath to the sea of the plain.</p><p>Archaeological evidence confirms the biblical account of his reign as the most prosperous the northern kingdom had known. In 1910 G. A. Reisner found 63 inscribed potsherds at the royal palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, later dated to his reign, which give information about the script, language, religion and administration of the period. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> seal found at Megiddo in 1904 reads Belonging to Shema, servant of Jeroboam. By the late 8th century BCE the territory of Israel was the most densely settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, with about 350,000 people, and its prosperity rested on trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, wine and possibly horses with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His reign was the period of the prophets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosea">Hosea</a>, Joel, Amos and Jonah, who condemned the materialism and selfishness of the Israelite elite. According to Amos, the king's triumphs bred boastful overconfidence, the oppression of the poor by the mighty and luxury in palaces. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Book of Kings</a> condemns him for doing evil in the eyes of the Lord, meaning both the oppression of the poor and his support of the cult centres at Dan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a>. The archaeologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israel-finkelstein">Israel Finkelstein</a> has argued that many stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s rule over a large, prosperous kingdom were inspired by memories of the reign of Jeroboam II.</p><p>The Book of Amos mentions a major earthquake in the days of Jeroboam son of Jehoash. It is dated to about 760 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yigael-yadin">Yigael Yadin</a> and Finkelstein dated the destruction level at Tel Hazor to it, as David Ussishkin did the destruction at Lachish. His name occurs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> only in 2 Kings, 1 Chronicles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosea">Hosea</a> and Amos; elsewhere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-i">Jeroboam I</a> is meant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jeroboam II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Hazael of Damascus the Arameans took much of Israel’s territory in the later ninth century, but Joash and <b>Jeroboam II</b> won it back.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.28,35.19&amp;year=-766" aria-label="Samaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5455 990 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Jeroboam II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5905 1260h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5905 1260h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.28,35.19&amp;year=-766">What was here in 766 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
